# Most Important Rule: What Goes Where

- **Source code** writes **How** — the mechanism, the logic.
- **Test code** writes **What** — the expected behavior/spec.
- **Commit log** writes **Why** — the motivation for the change.
- **Code comments** write **Why not** — rejected alternatives, non-obvious constraints.
- Two exceptions write **What** instead, in **Japanese** (unlike other code comments): (1) a **Repository**'s type/class and every one of its methods always get a one-line What comment (the only class-level comment requirement — other classes follow Why-not); (2) any **complex method** anywhere (non-obvious control flow, several branches, a non-trivial algorithm) gets a one-line What comment.

Do not put implementation narration in comments. Do not embed expected specs in source. Put change motivation in the commit message.
